1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Two functions of the same n: time asks how many steps, space asks how many cells are alive at once.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
sum(n) = n + sum(n − 1) holds n + 1 frames at once, so its space is Θ(n) even though each frame is constant.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
The loop version makes the same n calls and never has two frames alive: Θ(1) space.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
So: space is a peak, not a total. Find the worst instant, then count what is live at it.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Total space is the input plus everything the algorithm adds. Auxiliary space counts only the addition.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Every comparison sort has total space Θ(n), because the array is the input. That sentence separates nothing.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Auxiliary space separates them: insertion and heapsort hold a constant, merge sort holds n cells, quick sort holds its stack.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
When GATE writes "space complexity" it means total; when it writes "extra space" it means auxiliary. Read which one is asked.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
GATE CSE 2005 Q81a: foo(n) loops i from 0 to n − 1 and calls foo(i) inside the loop.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
The deepest chain is foo(n) → foo(n − 1) → … → foo(0): n + 1 frames alive together.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
It makes 2ⁿ calls in all — 256 of them at n = 8 — while at most 9 frames exist at any instant.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
So the space is Θ(n), not Θ(2ⁿ). Counting calls over time is a time measurement.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Naive Fibonacci calls itself twice per step. At n = 20 that is 21,891 calls.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Yet the stack never holds more than 20 frames: the right-hand call begins only after the left one returns.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Space is the height of the call tree, not the number of its nodes.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Time is 2·F(n + 1) − 1 and space is n. At n = 30 the ratio is about 89,751 to 1.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Merge sort on 1,024 keys recurses 11 levels deep: ⌈log₂ 1,024⌉ + 1.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Its buffer holds 1,024 cells and its stack holds 11 frames. The peak is 1,035 cells, not 11.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Peak = buffer + stack = n + ⌈log₂ n⌉ + 1, so the answer is Θ(n) — the buffer decides it.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
A shallow recursion does not mean a small space. Count both parts, then keep the larger.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Sorted input with the last element as pivot: every partition splits off exactly one key.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
At n = 64 the recursion goes 64 levels deep and makes 2,016 comparisons, which is 64·63/2.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
The array is sorted in place — but the stack is Θ(n). Both statements are true at once.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
With a median or random pivot the depth is ⌈log₂ n⌉ + 1, so Θ(log n). The pivot decides the space as well as the time.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
"In place" is used for three different claims, and a question can turn on which one is meant.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Strict O(1): insertion, selection, bubble, and heapsort with an iterative sift-down.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Loose O(log n): quick sort, and heapsort with a recursive sift-down. The array is never copied.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Merge sort is not in place for arrays — n cells — but is for linked lists, where the stack is all it needs.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
BFS holds a frontier. On this nine-vertex graph the widest layer holds 4 vertices, so the queue is Θ(V).

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
DFS holds a path. The deepest chain here is 6 vertices, and in general up to V.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Storing the graph: a matrix is V² = 81 cells here; a list is V + 2E = 25 slots.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
A graph question's space is the representation plus the frontier or the path, plus the visited set.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Memoising Fibonacci turns 2ⁿ calls into n, and costs n cells to remember them.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Longest common subsequence of two strings of 1,000: the full table is 1,000,000 cells; two rolling rows are 2,000.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Floyd–Warshall keeps V² distances: 10,000 cells at 100 vertices, and Θ(V³) time.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
So space is a lever, not a given. The same computation has several space answers; say which one you are quoting.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Step 1: fix the model — one cell is one word, one frame is a constant number of words.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Step 2: add what is live at the worst instant — buffers, frontier or path, and stack depth.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Step 3: separate input from auxiliary, and answer the one the question asked for.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
The four traps: calls counted over time, tree size for tree height, in place for constant, total for auxiliary.

